# Why the observed orders depart from 2 / 4 / 8 (evidence: results/fits.json `local_orders_k_to_k+1`, results/fits_mp60.json)

Fit rule: OLS of ln(error) on ln(n), points with float64 error > 1e-15 (primary; 1e-14 and 1e-13 in fits.json), order = -slope, 95% interval = slope ± t(0.975, npts-2)·stderr; reported only when npts >= 3.
Point counts (k used): see results/orders_table.md. Threshold sensitivity: only the 1/(1+25x^2) Simpson fit moves materially (5.39 / 5.66 / 6.01 for thresholds 1e-15 / 1e-14 / 1e-13, 9 / 8 / 7 points) because it is dominated by the pre-asymptotic points; exp GL has 2 points (none at 1e-13).

**exp(x)** – analytic and entire: trapezoid 2.000 for all 12 points, Simpson 3.99–4.00 until the error reaches ~1e-14, GL-4 8.00 for k=1->2 only: errors are 3.7e-12, 1.5e-14, then float64 noise (<=2.2e-16). The only departure is the float64 floor, not a convergence defect; the 60-digit run gives 7.999 (all 12 points), 8.000 (k>=7). The float64 GL fit has two points: no interval can be defined.

**1/(1+25x^2)** – analytic on [0,1] but with poles at ±i/5, so the asymptotic regime needs panel width h << 1/5 (n >> 5). Pairwise local orders: trapezoid 3.84, 5.77, 1.52, then 2.00 to two decimals from k>=4 (1.9992, 1.9998, 1.99995, ...; exactly 2.0 only at k=11->12); Simpson 3.26, 7.21, 10.96, then 4.13, 4.00 (k>=4); GL 7.65, 5.3, 14.5 and then the floor. At n = 2..8 the rules resolve the peak of the function with a few panels and the error is not yet a power law (it can fall much faster, e.g. Simpson 4.5e-9 at n=16 from 9.1e-6 at n=8; the trapezoid error changes sign between n=4 and n=8, which produces the 5.77 / 1.52 local orders, and the GL error changes sign between n=2 and n=4). The log-log fit across this region gives biased orders (trapezoid 2.29 [2.02, 2.55], Simpson 5.39 [4.57, 6.21], GL 8.76 [2.9, 14.6], 4 points -> very wide). The asymptotic orders 2, 4, 8 are recovered in the 60-digit data for k=7..12 (2.000, 4.000, 8.000). The fitted interval for GL includes 8; the intervals for trapezoid and Simpson exclude the theoretical value (OLS intervals assume a single power law, which does not hold here – the departure is bias, not noise).

**sqrt(x)** – f(x) = x^{1/2} has an unbounded derivative at x=0, so the Euler–Maclaurin / Peano error expansions with f'', f'''' or f^(8) do not apply. The error is dominated by the first panel [0,1/n]: its contribution scales as n^{-3/2} for every rule, so Simpson and GL-4 do not improve on the exponent (they improve the constant only). Measured: trapezoid 1.483 [1.477, 1.489] (local order drifts 1.43 -> 1.50 as n grows; trapezoid has the extra C·n^-2 term which biases the finite-n fit slightly low), Simpson 1.500, GL-4 1.500 (the intervals have width < 5e-4, i.e. they are essentially exact power laws; the GL order is 1.5, not 1). 60-digit values identical. No float64 floor is reached (smallest error 4.4e-9). This is the only function where the 2/4/8 hierarchy is lost entirely, and the departure is a property of the integrand, not of rounding.
